WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.620
Daniel and Elena own a repair company.

2
00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.960
They face several different problems if someone dies.

3
00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:10.380
An owner's family may inherit a business interest without wanting to

4
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:15.140
operate the company. Losing a key manager may interrupt revenue and

5
00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:16.940
require a costly replacement.

6
00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:21.260
The company may also want to reward and retain a valuable

7
00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:26.220
employee. Life insurance can play a role in each situation, but

8
00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:30.640
the owner, beneficiary and intended use of proceeds differ.

9
00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:32.740
Start with the business objective.

10
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:38.040
Then trace who owns the policy, whose life is insured and

11
00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.240
who receives the money.

12
00:00:40.074 --> 00:00:45.114
Daniel and Elena want the surviving owner to buy the deceased

13
00:00:45.114 --> 00:00:49.894
owner's business interest at an agreed value or under an agreed

14
00:00:49.894 --> 00:00:55.074
valuation method. The buy-sell agreement creates

15
00:00:55.074 --> 00:01:00.414
the purchase framework. Life insurance can supply money for

16
00:01:00.414 --> 00:01:05.754
the death-triggered purchase. The agreement and the policy are separate

17
00:01:05.754 --> 00:01:11.014
documents. A policy alone does not decide the share price,

18
00:01:11.434 --> 00:01:14.994
force a transfer or resolve every ownership issue.

19
00:01:15.874 --> 00:01:20.674
The business must coordinate the legal agreement, valuation, ownership

20
00:01:20.674 --> 00:01:24.334
structure and insurance amount.

21
00:01:25.194 --> 00:01:29.854
Review the arrangement when the business value or the owners change.

22
00:01:30.852 --> 00:01:34.972
In a simple two-owner cross-purchase arrangement, Elena owns and is

23
00:01:34.972 --> 00:01:36.892
beneficiary of a policy on Daniel.

24
00:01:37.412 --> 00:01:40.492
Daniel owns and is beneficiary of a policy on Elena.

25
00:01:41.172 --> 00:01:45.232
If Daniel dies and the policy pays, Elena receives the proceeds.

26
00:01:46.292 --> 00:01:49.452
She uses the money under the buy-sell agreement to purchase Daniel's

27
00:01:49.452 --> 00:01:52.312
interest from his estate or other lawful successor.

28
00:01:53.292 --> 00:01:55.572
The surviving owner is the buyer in this example.

29
00:01:55.972 --> 00:01:58.252
The family receives the purchase price for the interest.

30
00:01:58.252 --> 00:02:01.732
Do not assume that the deceased owner's family is also the

31
00:02:01.732 --> 00:02:03.492
beneficiary of the funding policy.

32
00:02:04.134 --> 00:02:09.354
Change the structure. The repair company owns and

33
00:02:09.354 --> 00:02:11.594
is beneficiary of policies on its owners.

34
00:02:12.774 --> 00:02:16.854
If Daniel dies, the company receives the covered proceeds and uses

35
00:02:16.854 --> 00:02:19.374
them under the agreement to buy back his interest.

36
00:02:20.314 --> 00:02:24.414
This is an entity-purchase arrangement, also called a stock-redemption

37
00:02:24.414 --> 00:02:26.634
arrangement in a corporate setting.

38
00:02:27.694 --> 00:02:31.914
The business, rather than Elena individually, makes the purchase.

39
00:02:32.554 --> 00:02:36.114
Do not choose between structures from a slogan about taxes.

40
00:02:37.094 --> 00:02:40.734
Their legal, valuation and tax consequences differ.

41
00:02:41.794 --> 00:02:44.674
The learning task is to follow the ownership and payment flow

42
00:02:44.674 --> 00:02:47.194
and recognize the arrangement described.

43
00:02:48.216 --> 00:02:53.056
Now suppose the company depends on Marcus, a specialist whose relationships

44
00:02:53.056 --> 00:02:57.196
and technical skills support important contracts.

45
00:02:58.016 --> 00:03:01.776
The company buys life coverage on Marcus to protect its own

46
00:03:01.776 --> 00:03:07.636
lawful economic interest, with the required application and consent process.

47
00:03:08.436 --> 00:03:13.576
The company generally owns the policy, pays the premiums and

48
00:03:13.576 --> 00:03:15.436
receives the covered death benefit.

49
00:03:15.996 --> 00:03:21.756
It may use the funds for recruiting, training, lost revenue or

50
00:03:21.756 --> 00:03:27.136
debt obligations. This does not automatically buy an ownership interest or

51
00:03:27.136 --> 00:03:28.876
pay a personal family benefit.

52
00:03:29.356 --> 00:03:33.156
Key-person coverage protects the business against its loss from a valuable

53
00:03:33.156 --> 00:03:38.134
person's death. For a separate retention goal, the company helps a

54
00:03:38.134 --> 00:03:42.974
valued executive obtain personally owned life insurance through a bonus

55
00:03:42.974 --> 00:03:48.434
arrangement. The executive owns the policy and chooses the beneficiary under

56
00:03:48.434 --> 00:03:52.934
its terms. The employer provides the agreed compensation support;

57
00:03:53.714 --> 00:03:57.554
that payment alone does not make the employer the policyowner.

58
00:03:58.374 --> 00:04:02.634
The arrangement can include tax-related bonus planning, which needs its own

59
00:04:02.634 --> 00:04:07.314
review. Keep the core distinction clear: a buy-sell

60
00:04:07.674 --> 00:04:12.754
funds an ownership purchase, key-person coverage protects the business,

61
00:04:13.794 --> 00:04:17.314
and an executive bonus supports employee-owned protection.

62
00:04:18.434 --> 00:04:22.414
Follow the stated objective and the actual contract roles.
